Article 32
Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (1) letter number constitutes services provided by companions to restore the traumatic condition of victims.
(1) Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by means of: (2)
a. providing guidance and counseling;
b. psychological recovery of victims;
c. accompanying victims in the family and community environment; and d. providing Safe Houses for victims of violence who require continuous handling. Special social rehabilitation for women victims of domestic violence and human trafficking is carried out by means of: (3)
a. providing guidance and counseling;
b. psychological recovery of victims;
c. accompanying victims in the family and community environment; d. providing training and skills education; and e. providing business capital assistance.
Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by Regional Apparatus having principal tasks and functions in the fields of: (4)
a. social affairs;
b. women's empowerment and child protection;
c. education;
d. health; and
e. mental and spiritual.
In addition to being implemented by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (4), social rehabilitation may also be implemented by the community or social service institutions. (5)

Part Three Rights of Victims
Article 15
Women and children victims of acts of violence shall receive the following rights: a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected; b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim; c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality, especially regarding their identity; f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right to assistance; and j. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process.

Article 16
Women and children victims of acts of violence, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 15, also obtain special rights, as follows:
a. the right to respect and full utilization for survival, growth and development;
the right to basic services;
the right to equal protection;
the right to be free from various stigma;
the right to obtain freedom;
the right to obtain empowerment; and the right to be tried based on restorative justice.

CHAPTER II PREVENTION OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN First Part Forms of Violence Against Women and Children Article
Forms of violence against women and children, include:
a.
c. d.
f.
physical violence;
psychological violence;
sexual violence; neglect;
exploitation; and/or other violence.
Article Physical violence as referred to in Article letter constitutes an act that results in pain accompanied by injury, wounds or disability to the body, miscarriage, fainting and/or causes death of women and/or children.
Article Psychological violence as referred to in Article letter constitutes an act that results in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and/or psychological suffering of women and/or children. Article Sexual violence as referred to in Article letter includes: a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. coercion of sexual relations in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/or c. coercion of sexual relations with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.

Article 26
Forms of services for victims as referred to in Article 24 letter b (1), are as follows:
a. services for women and children victims of acts of violence, include:
1. complaint services, consultation and counseling; accompaniment services;
health services;
legal aid services;
repatriation and social reintegration services; and 6. social rehabilitation services.
b. in addition to the forms of services regulated in letter a, special services are also provided which include: 1. pick-up services; and/or
2. medical and psychological referral services. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a and letter b, are implemented by Regional Apparatus (2) in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government.
Further provisions regarding the implementation of services (3) as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

